-- title:
--   Existence of the p-divisible group J[p^∞]
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative local ring, $J$ a scheme and $f : J \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ a morphism, and let $L$ be a `RelativeGroupLaw` for $f$: for every $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ a multiplication, unit and inversion on the set $\{\varphi : T \to J \mid \varphi\ \text{followed by}\ f = t\}$ of $T$-points of $J$ over $R$, satisfying associativity, both unit laws, left inverse, and naturality of the multiplication under composition with any $\psi : T' \to T$ over $\operatorname{Spec} R$; assume in addition (`hcomm`) that every such multiplication is commutative. Let $p, h \in \mathbb{N}$, and assume that for each $v$ the endomorphism `L.schemeNsmul (p ^ v)` of $J$ — the morphism underlying the $p^v$-fold product of the identity point $\mathrm{id}_J$ in $J(J)$ — is finite (`IsFinite`) and flat (`Flat`). Let $\Omega$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero which is an $R$-algebra, and assume that for each $v$ the set of $x \in J(\Omega)$ with $p^v x = 1$ has exactly $p^{vh}$ elements. The conclusion asserts the existence of a [`PDivisibleGroup R p h`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199), namely rings `G.level v` which are commutative cocommutative Hopf $R$-algebras, finite and free as $R$-modules, of $R$-rank $p^{vh}$, together with surjective coalgebra-algebra maps $\mathrm{level}(v+1) \to \mathrm{level}(v)$ whose kernels are the ideals generated by the image of the augmentation ideal under multiplication by $p^v$; and, for every $v$ and every commutative $R$-algebra $T$, a bijection $e_{v,T}$ from `G.Point T v`, the set of $R$-algebra maps $\mathrm{level}(v) \to T$ with its convolution group structure, onto the set of $x \in J(T)$ (points over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the structure map $R \to T$) with $p^v x = 1$, such that: $e_{v,T}$ carries the convolution product to `L.mul`; for every $R$-algebra map $a : T \to T'$ the morphism underlying $e_{v,T'}(\mathrm{pointMap}\,a\,v\,x)$ is $\operatorname{Spec}(a)$ followed by the morphism underlying $e_{v,T}(x)$; and $e_{v+1,T}(\mathrm{pointIncl}\,x)$ equals $e_{v,T}(x)$ as a point of $J$ over $T$.
--
--   This is the construction, in the Hopf-algebra formulation of $p$-divisible (Barsotti–Tate) groups used here, of $J[p^\infty] = (J[p^v])_v$ for a commutative group scheme over $R$ whose multiplication-by-$p^v$ maps are finite and flat, with the height read off from the number of $p^v$-torsion points over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero. It feeds the statement relating the $p$-divisible group of the Jacobian with its torsion subschemes, and thence the Galois-theoretic input to the modularity argument.
